<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian Somaliland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Somaliland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Italian Somaliland was an Italian colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. It was one of the earliest acquisitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> and later part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory before independence in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland.jpg" alt="Italian Somaliland" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Italian interest began with exploring societies founded from 1876. In 1889 two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> rulers sought protection from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by treaty: Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid">Yusuf Ali Kenadid</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a>, who signed on 8 February, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boqor-osman-mahamuud">Boqor Osman Mahamuud</a> of the Majeerteen Sultanate, who signed on 7 April. Each hoped for Italian support against rivals. The treaties promised that Italy would not interfere in their administration, in return for arms and an annual subsidy.</p><p>In the south the consul at Zanzibar, Vincenzo Filonardi, was charged with creating outposts on the Banaadir coast. The port of Adale was occupied in February 1891 and renamed Itala. In 1893 Filonardi and the Italian navy occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Merca</a>, Mogadishu, Barawa and Warsheekh, and Giumbo followed in 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> resistance marked the whole period. In November 1896 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-cecchi">Antonio Cecchi</a>, administrator for the Benadir Company, and thirteen others were killed in an ambush at Lafoole by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimaal">Biimaal</a> and Wa'daan fighters. A punitive expedition under Giorgio Sorrentino in 1897 destroyed villages and seized livestock. Further wars against the Biimaal and their allies in 1905 to 1908 extended Italian control over the whole Lower Shabelle region. The Dervish movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a> defeated an Italian advance at Hiraan in 1913.</p><p>After the First World War Britain ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jubaland">Jubaland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1925, and by the end of 1927 the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majeerteen">Majeerteen</a> had been annexed after a two-year campaign under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-maria-de-vecchi">Cesare Maria De Vecchi</a>. The colony saw railways, dams, farms and villages, among them an agricultural settlement at Jowhar. By 1935 more than 50,000 Italians lived there, about 5 per cent of the population. In October 1935 the southern front of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> was launched from the colony, and in June 1936 it became the Somalia Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>British forces defeated the Italians in 1941 and administered the territory until 1950. The Somali Youth Club, the first modern Somali political party, was founded in Mogadishu in 1943 and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-youth-league">Somali Youth League</a>. In November 1949 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> trusteeship on condition that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> become independent within ten years, and Italian administration began on 1 April 1950. The Somali Youth League won 75.58 per cent of the vote in the parliamentary election of 1959. On 1 July 1960 the Trust Territory united with former British Somaliland to form the Somali Republic, with Mogadishu as its capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian Somaliland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together with Massawa, occupied in 1885, this grew into the colony of Eritrea, formally proclaimed in 1890, and was followed by the gradual establishment of <b>Italian Somaliland</b> on the Horn of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 June 1960 British Somaliland became independent as the State of <b>Somaliland</b>, which five days later united with the former Trust Territory of Somaliland to form the Somali Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Somaliland's</b> later governments state that some 35 countries, including the United Kingdom, recognised it.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-somaliland">State of Somaliland</a></li></ul></section>
